===Legislative Assembly===
Although Solís' [[Citizens' Action Party (Costa Rica)|PAC]] received the most votes in the presidential elections,<ref name="Landsford">{{cite book|last1=Landsford|first1=Tom|title=Political Handbook of the World 2014|date=20 March 2014|isbn=9781483386263|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=iC_VBQAAQBAJ&q=costa+rica%27s+end+of+two+party+system&pg=PA333|access-date=13 March 2016|archive-date=2 February 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220202011712/https://books.google.com/books?id=iC_VBQAAQBAJ&q=costa+rica%27s+end+of+two+party+system&pg=PA333|url-status=live}}</ref> the party did not win in the parliamentary voting making [[National Liberation Party (Costa Rica)|PLN]] the largest party in the Assembly with 18 deputies over PAC's 13.<ref name="IPU">{{cite web|title=Asamblea Legislativa (Legislative Assembly)|url=http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2073_e.htm|website=IPU.org|access-date=13 March 2016|archive-date=13 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160313131351/http://www.ipu.org/parline-e/reports/2073_e.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

Leftist party [[Broad Front (Costa Rica)|Broad Front]] surprised with its results, achieving 9 seats,<ref name="Lehring">{{cite news|last1=Lehring|first1=Gary|title=Costa Rican legislative elections show growing voter dissatisfaction with traditional choices|url=http://www.ticotimes.net/2014/02/15/costa-rican-legislative-elections-show-growing-voter-dissatisfaction-with-traditional-choices|access-date=13 March 2016|agency=The Tico Times|date=February 15, 2014|archive-date=12 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160312144206/http://www.ticotimes.net/2014/02/15/costa-rican-legislative-elections-show-growing-voter-dissatisfaction-with-traditional-choices|url-status=live}}</ref> first time ever that the Left achieved such a big number.<ref name="IPU"/> [[Social Christian Unity Party]] recovered part of its former influence<ref name="Lehring"/> by turning into the fourth political party in legislative size even when its candidate Rodolfo Piza was fifth in the presidential vote.<ref name="ER2">{{cite web|title=February 7, 2010 Presidential Election Results - Costa Rica Totals|url=http://www.electionresources.org/cr/president.php?election=2014|website=Elections Resources|access-date=13 March 2016|archive-date=4 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160504150327/http://www.electionresources.org/cr/president.php?election=2014|url-status=live}}</ref> The opposite happened to [[Otto Guevara]]’s right-wing [[Libertarian Movement (Costa Rica)|Libertarian Movement]],<ref name="Lehring"/> fourth in presidential votes,<ref name="ER2"/> which stood fifth in legislative elections, and as a result, the number of its deputies was reduced from 9 to 4.<ref name="Lehring"/><ref name="IPU"/> Oscar Lopez’s [[Accessibility without Exclusion Party|PASE]] party also suffered a diminishment in number of deputies from 4 to 1 (Lopez himself).<ref name="Lehring"/><ref name="ER">{{cite web|title=February 2, 2014 Legislative Assembly Election Results - Costa Rica Totals|url=http://www.electionresources.org/cr/deputies.php?election=2014|access-date=13 March 2016|archive-date=4 May 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160504150244/http://www.electionresources.org/cr/deputies.php?election=2014|url-status=live}}</ref>

Three Christian parties, oriented toward the [[Protestantism|Protestant minority]]<ref>{{cite news|last1=Lopez|first1=Jaime|title=Civic Groups Move Against Gay Marriage in Costa Rica|url=http://news.co.cr/civic-groups-move-against-gay-marriage-in-costa-rica/23281/|access-date=13 March 2016|agency=Costa Rica Star|date=July 18, 2013|archive-date=13 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160313201515/http://news.co.cr/civic-groups-move-against-gay-marriage-in-costa-rica/23281/|url-status=live}}</ref> and very [[socially conservative]], also achieved deputies: [[Costa Rican Renewal Party]] 2, [[National Restoration Party (Costa Rica)|National Restoration]] 1 and [[Christian Democratic Alliance (Costa Rica)|Christian Democratic Alliance]] 1.<ref name="ER"/>

