====Candidates elected====
Fifty-seven legislators were elected and took office on 1 May 2014, eleven of whom had been members of the Legislative Assembly in the past. Five were from the National Liberation Party: Antonio Álvarez Desanti, Juan Luis Jiménez, Olivier Jiménez, Rolando González, and Sandra Piszk. Two were from the Citizen Action Party: Epsy Campbell and Ottón Solís. Mario Redondo of the Christian Democratic Alliance served previously with the Social Christian Unity Party. The others were Otto Guevara of the Libertarian Movement Party, Oscar López of Accessibility without Exclusion Party, and Jorge Rodríguez of the Social Christian Unity Party.<ref>[http://www.ticotimes.net/2014/05/01/11-lawmakers-return-to-costa-ricas-legislative-assembly-for-second-term 11 lawmakers return to Costa Rica's Legislative Assembly for second term] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140502030049/http://www.ticotimes.net/2014/05/01/11-lawmakers-return-to-costa-ricas-legislative-assembly-for-second-term |date=2014-05-02 }} The Tico Times, 2014-05-01.</ref> The full list is as follows:<ref>[http://www.asamblea.go.cr/Diputadas_Diputados/Documents/Declatoria_diputados_2014-2018.pdf Declaratoria de elección de Diputados a la Asamblea Legislativa de la República de Costa Rica 2014-2018] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304090239/http://www.asamblea.go.cr/Diputadas_Diputados/Documents/Declatoria_diputados_2014-2018.pdf |date=2016-03-04 }} Tribunal Supremo de Elecciones, 2014-03-03. {{in lang|es}}
